+## Accuracy with available inputs
+
+After analysis, the dashboard shows test accuracy for the **actual set of fields
+you supplied**. For each target, the backend uses its saved 61-patient test split:
+
+1. Keep the test patients' recorded values for the supplied fields.
+2. Replace omitted fields with the same server defaults used for your assessment.
+3. Run the frozen model and apply its saved classification threshold.
+4. Compare classifications with the known labels: **accuracy = correct / total**.
+
+For example, supplying only Age and Diabetes keeps those two columns on the
+test records and defaults the other 52. A result of 52 correct classifications
+out of 61 is 85.2% accuracy. This arithmetic example does not claim that those
+two fields achieve that score.
+
+| Display | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| Prediction probability | Model estimate calculated from this patient's values and any defaults |
+| Input coverage | Supplied fields / 54; explicit zero values count as supplied |
+| Test accuracy | Correct classifications on held-out records with the same supplied/missing fields |
+
+The panel also shows recall, 95% Wilson intervals, accuracy with all inputs
+available, and an always-guess baseline. Your entered values affect your
+prediction; the test score depends on which fields are present. Two submissions
+with the same field count can therefore have different accuracies. Adding fields
+does not guarantee an increase, and the score does not establish whether an
+individual prediction is correct.
+
+Empty submissions are flagged as defaults only. Form edits require another
+analysis; PDF exports retain the completed assessment's scores. The check does
+not retrain models or tune thresholds. See the
+[evaluation method and limitations](docs/evaluation.md#accuracy-with-available-inputs).
+
