+
+```bash
+python training/train.py --targets cad --learning-curves best  # default: winner
+python training/train.py --targets cad --learning-curves all   # every configuration
+python training/train.py --targets cad --learning-curves none  # disable plots
+python training/train.py --curves-only                        # saved winners, all targets
+```
+
+`--curves-only` reads each target's saved winning configuration and refits it
+on the first CV fold to reconstruct diagnostics. It leaves serving models,
+preprocessors, target metadata and evaluation metrics untouched. It creates a
+new timestamped curve directory labelled `regenerated_from_saved_config`;
+these are newly computed curves, not recovered historical training logs.
+Use `--targets cad` to limit this command to one target.
+
+Each curve directory stores:
+
+| File | Contents |
+|---|---|
+| `model.png` | Seven panels: log loss, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, accuracy, F1, Brier and F2; train and validation series |
+| `metrics.csv` | One row per component, fitted stage and split, including the threshold and all seven metrics |
+| `metadata.json` | Configuration, source fold/seed, row indices, sample counts, timestamp and plot filenames |
+| `<member>.png` | Member curves when the candidate is stacking or voting |
+
+Boosters record every fitted iteration. Random Forest records each cumulative
+tree prefix, labelled **Number of trees**. Voting and stacking have member
+curves plus a final ensemble score point; ElasticNet and other estimators
+without staged predictions get final-fit points rather than invented epochs.
+The ensemble's final point uses its own threshold; member curves use 0.5.
+F2 is computed with `fbeta_score(beta=2)` at the same threshold as F1 and
+accuracy. Plotting F2 does not change model selection, which still uses
+mean CV ROC-AUC with F1 as the tie-breaker.
+All exported metrics are unweighted diagnostics, so log loss can differ from
+a class-weighted training objective. Plots use headless Matplotlib, installed
+through `training/requirements.txt`; no GUI or backend plotting dependency is needed.
+
+`all` writes a configuration's curves after its CV scores finish, so completed
+configurations remain available if a later one fails. Separate runs never
+overwrite earlier curve directories. A process already running older code
+must finish or restart before this exporter can be used.
+
+### Regression checks
+
+Run from the repository root after installing the training dependencies:
+
+```bash
+python -m unittest discover -s training/tests -v
+```
+
+`test_voting.py` checks two-column probabilities, single-row predictions,
+threshold handling, missing member importances, and a voting winner's
+save/load, evaluation, and inference path. Its training integration check
+uses small estimators and a fixed tuning result in a temporary artifact folder.
+`test_lightgbm.py` treats prediction warnings as errors and checks native and
+voting/OOF predictions, unchanged probabilities, serialisation, and invalid
+inputs. These are focused regressions, not a full model-search rerun.
+`test_learning_curves.py` checks every model family's stage endpoints, PNG/CSV
+output, ensemble member handling, best/all/none modes, test-row exclusion and
+curve regeneration without overwriting serving artifacts.
+`test_ensemble_cv.py` checks inner and outer row isolation, learned statistics,
+stability/final-refit paths, and independent artifact serialization.
+`test_model_info.py` verifies voting-winner dashboard metadata.
+
+### Troubleshooting older runs
+
+- **`IndexError: too many indices for array` after the stack result:** the next
+  candidate is voting. Its old `predict_proba()` returned a one-dimensional
+  positive-probability array; the current version returns `(n_samples, 2)`.
+  Use the current code and rerun the training command. Completed configurations
+  remain in `experiments.csv`, but the script does not resume from that log.
+- **`X does not have valid feature names, but LGBMClassifier was fitted with
+  feature names`:** older plain LightGBM estimators warn when predicting from
+  arrays despite fitting on arrays. Current builders use `LGBMArrayClassifier`
+  to supply the fitted names; no global warning filter is needed. This warning
+  alone does not stop an existing run. A running process keeps its imported
+  code; the fix applies to new processes and newly fitted LightGBM artifacts.
+
